[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0m[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong and what I changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe problem:[22m The test runner dynamically loads the compiled Rust shared library ([38;5;10mlibsha2.so[0m) and looks up the symbol [38;5;10mSPX_prf_addr[0m to call it. This symbol didn't exist in the library.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mRoot cause:[22m In the original C code, all public functions are namespaced using a macro:[0m[0m
[1mc
[0m[38;5;10m#define SPX_NAMESPACE(s) SPX_##s
#define prf_addr SPX_NAMESPACE(prf_addr)  // expands to SPX_prf_addr
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
So the C library exports the symbol [38;5;10mSPX_prf_addr[0m. The Rust translation correctly implemented the [38;5;10mprf_addr[0m logic in [38;5;10mhash.rs[0m, but it was only a regular Rust function — it was never exported as a C-compatible symbol with the [38;5;10mSPX_prf_addr[0m name.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe fix:[22m Added a [38;5;10m#[no_mangle] pub extern "C"[0m wrapper function in [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10m#[unsafe(no_mangle)]
pub extern "C" fn SPX_prf_addr(
    out: *mut u8,
    ctx: *const context::SpxCtx,
    addr: *const [u32; 8],
) {
    unsafe {
        let out_s = std::slice::from_raw_parts_mut(out, SPX_N);
        hash::prf_addr(out_s, &*ctx, &*addr);
    }
}
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This bridges the C ABI expectation (the [38;5;10mSPX_prf_addr[0m symbol with raw pointer arguments) to the safe Rust implementation in [38;5;10mhash::prf_addr[0m. The underlying [38;5;10mprf_addr[0m implementation itself was correct — it properly computes [38;5;10mSHA-256(state_seeded || addr || sk_seed)[0m matching the C behavior. The only issue was the missing FFI export.[0m[0m
